    \u043f\u0443\u0431\u043b\u0438\u0447\u043d\u0443\u044e \u043a\u043e\u043b\u043a\u043e\u0441\u0442\u044c."
  interestingFactsEn: "Carl Weyerstrass' life is full of amazing paradoxes, funny times and touching details that show him not only as a dry academic, but also as a living, passionate, and very human person, and these are the brightest and most interesting facts, the chickens, and the details from his biography.\n\n1. A mathematician who was not afraid of scars (studies duells) Contrary to the image of a quiet study scientist, Weyerstrass was a desperate sorvevil during the Bonn period (1834-1838), and joined the Corps Guestphia Fellowship of Students and became famous as one of the best fencing artists of the university. Curiez: He participated in dozens of raspary duves, and his face was decorated by classic scramies (Schmiss) — the pride of German students at that time.Irony: In four years of violent student life, he was never seriously injured by rapir punches in the head and body, and years later, sitting in the silence of the office, he earned chronic headaches and insomnia from mental stress, so he was safer than math.\n\n2. The savings on stamps and \"disrupted fame\" during the years of teaching in Deutch Cron and Brownsberg (1843-1856), Weierstress was so poor that it was limited to the anecdot. The fact that his annual salary (348 talers) was barely enough to cover food and candles. To send his genius article on abel functions to the authoritative Journal of Krellé, he had to buy postage stamps.Weyerstrass had deliberately postponed the dispatch of manuscripts for months because he didn't have money for stamps for a heavy envelope, so he would have sent them out with bliss or debt, which was a major delay in publishing his discoveries.\n\n3. \"Professor with Small\" and lecture in the chair after 1861, Weyerstrass developed a heavy form of dizziness (vertigo) attacks. He could no longer stand on the chair. Traditional: Weyerstress sat in a big soft chair in front of the board and lectured sitting down. But since he himself could not climb up to the board, he dictated formulas, and the students went in turn to the board and wrote chalk under his dictation.Funny bark: Eyewitnesses remembered that he demanded that he write perfectly exactly. If a student curved an integral sign, Weyerstrassus would stop the lecture and say, \"A young man, math is not calligraphy, but the order of the signs is order in his head!\" And he would never raise the voice, and the students loved him.\n\n4. A dog who was walking instead of Professor Weyerstress loved animals, especially dogs. He had a taxi in Berlin. Curiez: On the slope of years when he was weak, he still wanted to walk, but because he was on the move, he invented a system: he lets the cab out in front of him on a leash, and the dog slowly dragged him down the alleys of Tiergaerten.Neighbors and students often saw the famous mathematician sticking to the leash, literally following his taxi, which chose its own route.\n\n5. How he \"destructed\" the intuition (Scandal with a function) in 1861, he gave his famous example of a function that was continuous everywhere but without derivative anywhere. The reaction: Contemporarys took it as a mathematical provocation. The famous mathematician Charles Ermit called it \"a terrible function\".One of Weyerstrass' students, when he heard the evidence, said, \"If it's true, I will never believe any more geometrical pictures!\" Weyerstress laughed and responded with a legendary phrase: \"There's no room for drawings in mathematics, there's only room for strict definitions.\n\n6. Burned correspondence (Act of a Secret) after receiving news of the sudden death of Sophia Kovalevskaya in 1891, Weyerstress committed a strange and mystical act. Curiez (dramatical): He collected all the letters Kovalevsky had ever written to him (and there were hundreds of them full of personal distress) and burned them to the fireplace, and carefully preserved all his letters sent to her.Why would he do that? The biographers assume that he wanted to take the secret of their true relationship to the grave so that no one would read its revelations.\n\n7. In 1885, in honour of 70 years, the German government wanted to take away the gold medal in honour of Weyerstrass. Curiez: Weyerstress refused! He said he didn't want his portrait to be scratched on the money. Instead of the medal, he asked for a simple copper globe of the star sky so he could look at the constellation lying in bed.The present was made, and this globe stood by his bed until his death.\n\n8. While he was already chained to bed in recent years (1894 - 1897), he did not stop teaching. The detail: He gave lectures at his home. The students (the 10 - 15 man) sat around his bed with notebooks, and he, lying on pillows and staring at the ceiling, directed them to the most difficult theorems. If he was interested in and drew out a cumbersome formula, he would use his finger to air over himself, as if he was peeing on an invisible board.The disciples called it a \"milk finger,\" and Weyerstrass never lost his way or was wrong about the long line.\n\n9. He had a long-standing conflict with a colleague, Leopold Cronecker, who came to the point where they stopped bowing at a meeting in the halls of the university. Curiez: They said that one day at the Weyerstrassa Bunt, he called a waiter and said loudly to hear Kroneker, \"Bring me a bottle of wine, only please, without irrational numbers in the amount of milliliters.\"Croneker responded with a demonstration, and this was one of the rare cases where Weyerstress had a sarcastic public cleavage."
